Talk, User, File and corresponding talk namespaces remain runtime MediaWiki surfaces but are not automatically deployed from this public corpus. Their omission from a deployable source tree is deliberate and different from pretending they do not exist.

== Primitive responsibilities ==

Use the smallest native primitive whose semantics match the job.

{| class="wikitable"
! Need
! Preferred primitive
! Do not substitute
|-
| Independent canonical knowledge
| Page / knowledge object
| Tiny fragments created solely for architectural symmetry
|-
| Repeated declarative rendering or metadata emission
| Template
| Copy-pasted wikitext
|-
| Non-trivial reusable logic or validation
| Scribunto/Lua Module
| Deep nests of ParserFunctions
|-
| Graph assertion
| SMW Property
| Category membership or prose when a stable relationship is intended
|-
| Reusable computed semantic set
| SMW Concept
| Hand-maintained list that is supposed to update from metadata
|-
| Qualified/nested semantic fact
| SMW subobject when the fact genuinely has its own attributes
| Flattening a relationship until meaning is lost
|-
| Repeated structured records with table/query/drilldown needs
| Cargo
| Cargo tables invented for one-off page metadata
|-
| Human browse hierarchy
| Category
| Ontology claims inferred from navigation alone
|-
| Reuse of exact authored material
| MediaWiki transclusion
| Duplicate prose
|-
| Relationship-driven exploration
| SMW queries / Network / faceted views
| Static lists that silently drift
|}

This division is a default, not a prohibition against combination. A template may emit semantic properties; a Lua module may support a template; a category may contain an SMW query; a Concept may feed another query. Composition is valuable when each layer retains a clear job.

== Compatibility template recovery ==

The live V1 fidelity inventory contains nine templates. They are represented directly in <code>Template/</code> so a reader does not have to excavate the provenance archive to discover runtime dependencies.

This recovery does '''not''' declare every V1 visual template part of the V2 design language.

* <code>Template:Infobox</code> is a high-impact compatibility dependency with 80 recorded callers.
* The BITwiki decorative/header/footer templates have live callers and remain compatibility surfaces until those callers are deliberately migrated.
* New V2 templates such as <code>Template:Knowledge object</code> are the canonical path for new V2 knowledge objects.
* A live compatibility template may be retired only after its callers and rendered behavior have been verified.

See <code>Template/README.md</code> and <code>archive-v1/templates/</code>.

== Lua: mature the primitive, not the old vocabulary ==

V1 specified a larger Lua validation architecture, but the live Module namespace was empty in the captured corpus. The specification is therefore design evidence, not proof that five production modules existed.

V2 begins with one bounded module: <code>Module:Structure</code>. Its first responsibility is runtime validation of the explicit identity supplied to <code>Template:Knowledge object</code>. This preserves a useful V1 idea — reusable validation — while using the current V2 controlled vocabularies and avoiding the obsolete numeric-confidence model.

The module does not manufacture metadata. Valid calls produce no extra visible content; malformed direct wiki edits produce a validation message and maintenance category. Repository CI remains the pre-deployment validator, while Lua provides a runtime backstop for edits made through MediaWiki.

== Semantic MediaWiki ==

SMW is a substrate, not an infobox decoration.

Current V2 use should progressively exercise four distinct layers:

# '''Assertions''' — stable properties emitted by knowledge objects and deliberate relationship annotations.
# '''Queries''' — <code>#ask</code> views that derive navigation from those assertions.
# '''Concepts''' — named reusable semantic sets when more than one consumer benefits from the same query.
# '''Integrity''' — WantedProperties, UnusedProperties, processing/constraint errors and Browse/Ask diagnostics used as maintenance surfaces.

<code>Concept:Computer science</code> is the first V2 proof of the third layer: a dynamic set defined by <code>[[Domain::Computer science]]</code>. It is not a new taxonomy branch.

== Categories as composed navigation objects ==

A substantive Domain category is not required to duplicate a large block of wikitext in every source file. <code>Template:Knowledge domain category</code> provides shared structure and a live SMW query while each Category page supplies domain-specific scope and boundary text.

A short category source file is therefore not automatically a stub. The relevant test is the rendered and semantic function:

* Does it explain scope?
* Does it state meaningful boundaries?
* Does it expose actual knowledge objects dynamically?
* Does it connect to relevant navigation?
* Does it avoid claiming ontology from category placement alone?

Administrative categories remain intentionally concise.

== Cargo: one bounded operational proof ==

Cargo now has one real consumer: the repeated records in [[BITwiki:Requested knowledge]]. [[Template:Knowledge request]] declares and stores the <code>Knowledge_requests</code> table, while the queue page uses <code>#cargo_query</code> for a computed lifecycle view.

This is deliberately not page-identity duplication. Request records are project-operational objects with repeated fields and mutable workflow state; canonical knowledge remains modeled through pages, SMW properties, relationships, and provenance.

The request workflow also demonstrates why implementation should follow a use case rather than an extension checklist:

<pre>
missing coverage signal
→ authored Knowledge request record
→ Cargo storage/query
→ research lifecycle
→ canonical page only when ready
→ satisfied request links canonical result
</pre>

<code>scripts/audit_workflow.py</code> validates the Cargo declaration/store contract and enforces the critical governance invariant that an active request cannot simultaneously exist as a canonical Main page.

The Cargo database table itself is runtime state and must be explicitly created/recreated after deploying the template; Git source alone cannot prove that remote database step. See [[BITwiki:Deployment prerequisites]].

== Subobjects and additional exploration remain evidence-gated ==

SMW subobjects, additional Concepts, Network and faceted/drilldown surfaces remain available capabilities, but architecture quality is not measured by turning every extension on everywhere.

Subobjects should appear when a fact needs its own attributes, provenance or qualification. Additional Concepts should appear when repeated query logic has more than one useful consumer. Network/faceted surfaces should appear when they improve an actual exploration workflow. Until those cases exist, a count of zero is an honest result rather than an implementation failure.

== Measurable substrate use ==

Run:

<pre>
python scripts/audit_substrate.py
python scripts/audit_workflow.py
</pre>

The audits report:

* configured namespaces and source-control mappings;
* current live templates missing from deployable <code>Template/</code>;
* <code>#ask</code>, <code>#concept</code>, <code>#invoke</code>, <code>#subobject</code> and Cargo primitive counts;
* semantic property assertion counts;
* transclusion markup use;
* mapped roots or directories missing README orientation;
* Cargo request schema/storage/query agreement;
* request lifecycle integrity and active-request/canonical-page conflicts.

The goal is to make architectural claims falsifiable. A page saying “BITwiki uses Lua” is weak evidence; source-controlled modules plus real <code>#invoke</code> consumers are stronger evidence. Likewise, “Cargo is installed” is not a workflow; a declared table, stored records, queries, deployment procedure and invariant tests are.

Generated audit JSON is commit-specific evidence. CI uploads it as an artifact; it is intentionally not committed back to <code>main</code>.

== Deployment order ==

When the substrate is imported into MediaWiki, dependencies should be satisfied before consumers:

<pre>
MediaWiki / SMW schema prerequisites
→ Property definitions
→ Module logic
→ foundational Templates
→ compatibility Templates
→ create/recreate required Cargo tables
→ Concepts
→ Categories / navigation views
→ knowledge objects and operational consumers
→ semantic/Cargo data verification
</pre>

Exact deployment remains additive and non-destructive. See [[BITwiki:Deployment prerequisites]].

